<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pocahontas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1596–<a href="/years/1617">1617</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Rebecca Rolfe, Matoaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pocahontas was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahunsenacah">Wahunsenacah</a>, paramount chief of the Powhatan, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. Captured by the English in 1613, she converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, took the name Rebecca and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-rolfe">John Rolfe</a> in 1614, a match that brought a brief peace. She travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1616 and died at Gravesend in 1617.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pocahontas.jpg" alt="Pocahontas" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simon van de Passe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pocahontas_by_Simon_van_de_Passe_(1616).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pocahontas was born Amonute and was also known as Matoaka; Pocahontas was a childhood nickname that the colonist William Strachey translated as little wanton. Her birth year is unknown, with historians estimating about 1596. Her father was Powhatan, the Mamanatowick of the Powhatan Confederacy, an alliance of about thirty polities in the Tidewater region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. Her mother's name and origin are unknown. Although popular culture often calls her a princess, she was not in line to succeed her father, since Powhatan's brothers, sisters and his sisters' children stood in line to succeed him.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-smith-explorer">John Smith</a> arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> with 100 other settlers in April 1607. Pocahontas befriended the colonists and played games with the boys at the settlement, and early accounts say she brought food that saved many from starving. Smith's well-known story of her saving him from execution appears only in his 1616 letter to Queen Anne and his 1624 Generall Historie, and scholars dispute it: Karen Ordahl Kupperman sees embroidery, while Frederic W. Gleach suggests Smith misunderstood a ritual of adoption.</p><p>In March 1613, during the First Anglo-Powhatan War, Captain Samuel Argall learned that she was visiting the Patawomeck village of Passapatanzy. With the help of the weroance Iopassus he lured her onto his ship, the Treasurer, and held her for ransom. At Henricus the minister Alexander Whitaker taught her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and she took the name Rebecca. She married the tobacco planter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-rolfe">John Rolfe</a> on 5 April 1614, and their son Thomas was born in January 1615. Their marriage began eight years of peace between the colonists and the Powhatan, known as the Peace of Pocahontas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The London Company brought the Rolfes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where they arrived at Plymouth on 12 June 1616, to promote investment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. She was presented to English society as a princess, attended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ben-jonson">Ben Jonson</a>'s masque The Vision of Delight at Whitehall on 5 January 1617, and met Smith, who had been thought dead in Virginia. In March 1617 the Rolfes boarded a ship to return, but she fell gravely ill at Gravesend and died of unknown causes at about 21. She was buried on 21 March 1617 at St George's Church, whose later destruction by fire in 1727 left the grave's exact location unknown.</p><p>Many of the stories told of her have been romanticised, and contemporary sources support her friendship with Smith but not a romance, which first appears in 1803. Her descendants run through Thomas Rolfe and his daughter Jane. In 1907 she was the first Native American honoured on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> stamp.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pocahontas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wahunsenacah’s daughter <b>Pocahontas</b>, whose own names were Amonute and Matoaka, was captured in 1613, baptised Rebecca, married Rolfe and died at Gravesend in England in 1617.</blockquote><a href="/empire/powhatan-confederacy">Powhatan Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werowocomoco">Werowocomoco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Gravesend, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.41,-76.65&amp;year=1609" aria-label="Werowocomoco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2633 817 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pocahontas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3083 1087h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3083 1087h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.41,-76.65&amp;year=1609">What was here in 1609? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
